--- ws-whatwg-c -- WHATWG WebSocket client and server over the
--- generated libc/libssl C ABIs.
---
--- @details
--- Transport layer above `ws_frame`: socket I/O is `~[1]~>` effectful
--- arrows over libc adapters; handshake/sockaddr/head logic is pure `~>`.
--- Entropy enters through libc `getrandom(2)` via the generated
--- `libc-c-linux-x86_64/random.hnl` adapter -- masking stays a
--- caller-supplied input to the pure codec, never an ambient effect.
---
--- Laws are `~[2]~>` 2-cell terms quantified over impl arrows, as in
--- `compress`. Only pure helpers carry laws -- effectful composition
--- across a network boundary is not a morphism equation.
---
--- Scope: `ws://` and `wss://` URLs (TLS via the generated
--- `libssl-c-linux-x86_64/ssl.hnl` adapter), IPv4-literal hosts with
--- `getaddrinfo` DNS fallback, a blocking `listen`/`accept` server
--- half in `server.hnl`. `wss://` sends SNI and verifies the peer
--- certificate chain and hostname against the default CA store
--- (`SSL_CERT_FILE`/`SSL_CERT_DIR` honoured). No IPv6, no redirects,
--- no permessage-deflate.
---
--- Blocking asymmetry: `recv` on a plain `ws://` conn polls with
--- `MSG_DONTWAIT`, but on a `wss://` conn it calls `SSL_read`, which
--- BLOCKS -- `MSG_DONTWAIT` does not reach through the TLS record
--- layer. Use `recv_wait` when blocking semantics are wanted on both
--- (or call `recv` knowing `wss` already blocks).

from "bytes" import { Bytes }

--- An open connection: the socket fd plus `rbuf`, the receive buffer
--- of undecoded bytes already read off the wire. `ssl` is the TLS
--- session handle for `wss://` conns (null on plain `ws://`), and
--- `ssl_ctx` the `SSL_CTX` it was created from -- the context must
--- outlive the session, so both ride on the conn for `close` to free
--- in order. `server` marks the `accept`ed half of a server
--- connection: server sends are unmasked (RFC 6455 section 5.1 --
--- clients mask, servers must not).
export Conn := { fd: Int, rbuf: Bytes, ssl: Value, ssl_ctx: Value, server: Bool }

--- A packed socket address as a record (what `sockaddr_out` returns).
export Sockaddr := { port: Int, ip: String }

--- sockaddr_in pack signature: (port, dotted-quad) ~> 16 wire bytes.
export SockaddrPack := (Int, String) ~> Bytes

--- sockaddr_in unpack signature: 16 wire bytes ~> {port, ip}.
export SockaddrUnpack := (Bytes) ~> Sockaddr

--- The identity arrow, named so laws can mention it.
export id := (x) ~> x

--- sockaddr_record -- rebuild the `{port, ip}` record out of `pack`'s
--- input pair. Named so the roundtrip law has an honest right side:
--- `pack >>> unpack` maps (port, ip) to a *record*, not back to the
--- input tuple, so `id` is not the correct codomain arrow here.
export sockaddr_record := (port, ip) ~> ({ port: port, ip: ip })

--- unpack is a left inverse of pack: decoding what a conforming packer
--- produced returns the input address as a record. Quantified over the
--- contract signatures so any implementation can be checked against it.
export sockaddr_roundtrip := (pack: SockaddrPack, unpack: SockaddrUnpack) ~> (pack >>> unpack) ~[2]~> sockaddr_record
